Lab 16: Automating Maintenance Tasks with Ansible
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Create and execute Ansible playbooks for Red Hat Satellite maintenance tasks
Automate backup processes for Red Hat Satellite using Ansible
Implement automated patch management workflows with Ansible
Test and validate automated maintenance processes using a repeatable testing playbook
Apply best practices for writing portable, idempotent Ansible automation in enterprise environments
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with YAML syntax and structure
Basic knowledge of Red Hat Satellite 6 concepts (services, Pulp content, Foreman database)
Understanding of SSH key-based authentication
Text editor experience (vi/vim or nano)
This lab uses a pre-configured environment where Ansible is already installed. If you are running this in your own practice environment instead, install Ansible on the control node first:

sudo dnf install -y ansible
Lab Environment
Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment. No need to build your own virtual machines or install software.

Your lab environment includes:

Control Node: CentOS/RHEL 8 with Ansible pre-installed
Satellite Server: Red Hat Satellite 6 simulation environment
Managed Nodes: Client systems for testing patch workflows
All necessary SSH keys and network connectivity pre-configured
All work in this lab is performed against your own isolated lab inventory. The playbooks are written defensively (idempotent, safe to re-run, with failed_when: false used only where a non-zero return code is an expected, non-fatal condition) so you can practice enterprise automation patterns safely.

Task 1: Build the Project Structure and a Satellite Health Check Playbook
Subtask 1.1: Verify Ansible and Prepare the Project
Confirm Ansible is available on the control node:
ansible --version
Expected result: version output showing ansible [core X.Y.Z] or similar, with no errors.

Create the project directory structure:
mkdir -p ~/satellite-automation/{playbooks,inventory,group_vars,templates}
cd ~/satellite-automation
Create the inventory file:
cat > inventory/satellite_hosts << 'EOF'
[satellite_servers]
satellite.example.com ansible_host=192.168.1.100 ansible_user=root

[satellite_clients]
client1.example.com ansible_host=192.168.1.101 ansible_user=root
client2.example.com ansible_host=192.168.1.102 ansible_user=root

[all:vars]
ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/id_rsa
ansible_ssh_common_args='-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no'
EOF
Test connectivity to all hosts in the inventory:
ansible all -i inventory/satellite_hosts -m ping
Deliverable: A working ~/satellite-automation directory tree and an inventory file. Expected result: each host returns "ping": "pong" with SUCCESS status (or, if the simulated hosts are unreachable in your specific environment, a clear connection message identifying the host â either way, confirm you understand the pass/fail output format before proceeding).

Subtask 1.2: Create a Satellite Health Check Playbook
Create the health check playbook:
cat > playbooks/satellite_health_check.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Red Hat Satellite Health Check
  hosts: satellite_servers
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Check Satellite services status
      systemd:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: started
      register: service_status
      failed_when: false
      loop:
        - foreman
        - httpd
        - postgresql
        - pulp_workers
        - pulp_celerybeat
        - pulp_resource_manager

    - name: Display service status
      debug:
        msg: "Service {{ item.item }} status check returned: {{ item.state | default('unknown') }}"
      loop: "{{ service_status.results }}"

    - name: Check disk space on Pulp content storage
      command: df -h /var/lib/pulp
      register: disk_space
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display disk usage
      debug:
        msg: "Pulp storage usage: {{ disk_space.stdout }}"

    - name: Check Satellite maintenance status
      command: satellite-maintain service status
      register: satellite_status
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display Satellite status
      debug:
        msg: "{{ satellite_status.stdout | default('satellite-maintain not available in this environment') }}"

    - name: Check active database connections
      become_user: postgres
      command: psql -c "SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;"
      register: db_connections
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display database connection count
      debug:
        msg: "Active database connections: {{ db_connections.stdout | default('unavailable') }}"
EOF
Run the health check playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/satellite_health_check.yml
Deliverable: A completed play run with a PLAY RECAP showing ok= tasks for satellite.example.com and no unreachable or fatal errors. Expected result: debug output displaying service status, disk usage, Satellite status, and database connection count (values may show as "unavailable" in a simulated environment, which is an acceptable pass since failed_when: false prevents the play from failing on those informational checks).

Task 2: Automate a Satellite Backup Workflow
Subtask 2.1: Define Backup Variables and the Manifest Template
Create backup configuration variables:
cat > group_vars/satellite_servers.yml << 'EOF'
backup_base_dir: /var/satellite-backups
backup_retention_days: 30
backup_timestamp: "{{ ansible_date_time.year }}{{ ansible_date_time.month }}{{ ansible_date_time.day }}_{{ ansible_date_time.hour }}{{ ansible_date_time.minute }}"
backup_dir: "{{ backup_base_dir }}/backup_{{ backup_timestamp }}"

backup_database: true
backup_configuration: true

notification_email: admin@example.com
smtp_server: localhost
EOF
Create the backup manifest template:
cat > templates/backup_manifest.j2 << 'EOF'
Red Hat Satellite Backup Manifest
=================================

Backup Information:
- Server: {{ inventory_hostname }}
- Backup Date: {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
- Backup Directory: {{ backup_dir }}
- Ansible User: {{ ansible_user }}

System Information:
- OS: {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}
- Kernel: {{ ansible_kernel }}
- Architecture: {{ ansible_architecture }}
- Total Memory: {{ ansible_memtotal_mb }}MB

Backup Components:
- Database Backup: {{ backup_database }}
- Configuration Backup: {{ backup_configuration }}

Restore Instructions:
1. Stop Satellite services
2. Restore database: gunzip -c foreman_db_backup.sql.gz | sudo -u postgres psql foreman
3. Restore configuration: tar -xzf satellite_config_backup.tar.gz -C /
4. Start Satellite services
EOF
Subtask 2.2: Create and Run the Backup Playbook
Create the backup playbook:
cat > playbooks/satellite_backup.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Red Hat Satellite Backup Automation
  hosts: satellite_servers
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes
  vars_files:
    - ../group_vars/satellite_servers.yml

  pre_tasks:
    - name: Create backup directory
      file:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
        owner: root
        group: root

    - name: Check available disk space on backup volume
      shell: "df -BG {{ backup_base_dir }} | awk 'NR==2 {print $4}' | sed 's/G//'"
      register: available_space
      changed_when: false

    - name: Warn if disk space is low (minimum 10GB recommended)
      debug:
        msg: "WARNING: Only {{ available_space.stdout }}GB available in {{ backup_base_dir }}"
      when: available_space.stdout | int < 10

  tasks:
    - name: Stop Satellite services for a consistent backup
      systemd:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: stopped
      loop:
        - foreman
        - httpd
        - pulp_workers
        - pulp_celerybeat
        - pulp_resource_manager
      register: stopped_services
      failed_when: false

    - name: Create Foreman database backup
      become_user: postgres
      shell: "pg_dump foreman > {{ backup_dir }}/foreman_db_backup.sql"
      when: backup_database
      register: foreman_dump
      failed_when: false

    - name: Create Candlepin database backup
      become_user: postgres
      shell: "pg_dump candlepin > {{ backup_dir }}/candlepin_db_backup.sql"
      when: backup_database
      register: candlepin_dump
      failed_when: false

    - name: Backup Satellite configuration files
      archive:
        path:
          - /etc/foreman
          - /etc/pulp
          - /etc/httpd/conf.d
        dest: "{{ backup_dir }}/satellite_config_backup.tar.gz"
        format: gz
      when: backup_configuration
      failed_when: false

    - name: Compress Foreman database dump
      archive:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}/foreman_db_backup.sql"
        dest: "{{ backup_dir }}/foreman_db_backup.sql.gz"
        format: gz
        remove: yes
      when: backup_database
      failed_when: false

    - name: Compress Candlepin database dump
      archive:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}/candlepin_db_backup.sql"
        dest: "{{ backup_dir }}/candlepin_db_backup.sql.gz"
        format: gz
        remove: yes
      when: backup_database
      failed_when: false

    - name: Create backup manifest file
      template:
        src: ../templates/backup_manifest.j2
        dest: "{{ backup_dir }}/backup_manifest.txt"

    - name: Calculate backup size
      command: "du -sh {{ backup_dir }}"
      register: backup_size
      changed_when: false

    - name: Start Satellite services
      systemd:
        name: "{{ item.item }}"
        state: started
      loop: "{{ stopped_services.results }}"
      when: item.changed | default(false)
      failed_when: false

    - name: Remove backups older than retention period
      find:
        paths: "{{ backup_base_dir }}"
        age: "{{ backup_retention_days }}d"
        file_type: directory
        patterns: "backup_*"
      register: old_backups

    - name: Delete expired backup directories
      file:
        path: "{{ item.path }}"
        state: absent
      loop: "{{ old_backups.files }}"

    - name: Display backup completion summary
      debug:
        msg: "Backup completed at {{ backup_dir }}. Size: {{ backup_size.stdout }}"
EOF
Run the backup playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/satellite_backup.yml
Deliverable: A backup directory created under /var/satellite-backups/backup_<timestamp> containing backup_manifest.txt and, where the simulated services allow it, the compressed database and configuration archives. Expected result: the final debug task prints the backup directory path and size, and the PLAY RECAP shows no fatal failures.

Task 3: Automate Patch Management for Satellite and Client Systems
Subtask 3.1: Create the Satellite Server Patch Playbook
Create shared patch management variables:
cat > group_vars/all.yml << 'EOF'
patch_management:
  reboot_required: true

exclude_packages:
  - kernel*
  - satellite*
  - foreman*

reboot_timeout: 600
reboot_delay: 30
EOF
Create the Satellite server patch playbook:
cat > playbooks/satellite_patch_management.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Satellite Server Patch Management
  hosts: satellite_servers
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes
  vars_files:
    - ../group_vars/all.yml

  tasks:
    - name: Get list of available updates
      yum:
        list: updates
      register: available_updates

    - name: Display available updates count
      debug:
        msg: "{{ available_updates.results | length }} updates available"

    - name: Create patch report directory
      file:
        path: /var/log/satellite-patches
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

    - name: Stop Satellite services before patching
      systemd:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: stopped
      loop:
        - foreman
        - httpd
        - pulp_workers
        - pulp_celerybeat
        - pulp_resource_manager
      register: stopped_services_patch
      failed_when: false

    - name: Update system packages, excluding protected packages
      yum:
        name: "*"
        state: latest
        exclude: "{{ exclude_packages | join(',') }}"
        update_cache: yes
      register: patch_result

    - name: Start Satellite services after patching
      systemd:
        name: "{{ item.item }}"
        state: started
      loop: "{{ stopped_services_patch.results }}"
      when: item.changed | default(false)
      failed_when: false

    - name: Check if a reboot is required
      stat:
        path: /var/run/reboot-required
      register: reboot_required_file

    - name: Reboot server if required
      reboot:
        reboot_timeout: "{{ reboot_timeout }}"
        pre_reboot_delay: "{{ reboot_delay }}"
        msg: "Rebooting for kernel updates"
      when:
        - patch_management.reboot_required
        - reboot_required_file.stat.exists or patch_result.changed

    - name: Display patch summary
      debug:
        msg: "Packages changed: {{ patch_result.changed }}. Reboot required: {{ reboot_required_file.stat.exists }}"
EOF
Subtask 3.2: Create the Client Systems Patch Playbook
The client reboot check below uses bash-specific [ -f ... ] test syntax, so the shell task explicitly requests /bin/bash as the execution shell to guarantee portable behavior regardless of the managed node's default shell.

Create the client patch management playbook:
cat > playbooks/client_patch_management.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Client Systems Patch Management
  hosts: satellite_clients
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes
  serial: 2
  vars_files:
    - ../group_vars/all.yml

  pre_tasks:
    - name: Check system connectivity
      ping:

  tasks:
    - name: Create client patch log directory
      file:
        path: /var/log/client-patches
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

    - name: Check for available updates
      yum:
        list: updates
      register: client_updates

    - name: Skip remaining tasks if no updates available
      meta: end_host
      when: client_updates.results | length == 0

    - name: Update all packages
      yum:
        name: "*"
        state: latest
        update_cache: yes
      register: client_patch_result

    - name: Check if reboot is required
      shell: |
        if [ -f /var/run/reboot-required ]; then
          echo "reboot_required"
        else
          echo "no_reboot_needed"
        fi
      args:
        executable: /bin/bash
      register: reboot_check
      changed_when: false

    - name: Generate post-patch report
      copy:
        dest: "/var/log/client-patches/post-patch-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.log"
        content: |
          Post-Patch Report - {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
          Hostname: {{ ansible_hostname }}
          Packages Updated: {{ client_patch_result.changed }}
          Reboot Status: {{ reboot_check.stdout }}

    - name: Reboot client if required
      reboot:
        reboot_timeout: "{{ reboot_timeout }}"
        pre_reboot_delay: "{{ reboot_delay }}"
        msg: "Rebooting after patch installation"
      when:
        - patch_management.reboot_required
        - reboot_check.stdout == "reboot_required"

    - name: Verify system is reachable after reboot
      wait_for_connection:
        delay: 30
        timeout: 300
      when:
        - patch_management.reboot_required
        - reboot_check.stdout == "reboot_required"
EOF
Run both patch playbooks against the lab inventory:
ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/satellite_patch_management.yml
ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/client_patch_management.yml
Deliverable: Both playbooks complete with a PLAY RECAP showing no fatal errors. Expected result: the Satellite play prints a patch summary debug message, and the client play either reports "no updates available" (via meta: end_host) or writes a post-patch report file to /var/log/client-patches/.

Task 4: Test and Validate the Automated Processes
Subtask 4.1: Build a Testing Playbook
This playbook validates the health check, backup, and patch automation by checking observable outcomes: service reachability, backup artifact existence, and log file creation.

Create the testing playbook:
cat > playbooks/test_automation.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Test Satellite Automation Processes
  hosts: satellite_servers
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    test_results: []

  tasks:
    - name: Test 1 - Verify Ansible connectivity
      ping:
      register: connectivity_test

    - name: Record connectivity test result
      set_fact:
        test_results: "{{ test_results + [{'test': 'Connectivity Test', 'status': 'PASS' if connectivity_test.ping is defined and connectivity_test.ping == 'pong' else 'FAIL'}] }}"

    - name: Test 2 - Check Satellite services
      systemd:
        name: "{{ item }}"
      register: service_test
      loop:
        - foreman
        - httpd
        - postgresql
      failed_when: service_test.status is not defined

    - name: Record service check test result
      set_fact:
        test_results: "{{ test_results + [{'test': 'Satellite Services Present', 'status': 'PASS' if service_test.results | selectattr('status', 'defined') | list | length == 3 else 'FAIL'}] }}"

    - name: Test 3 - Verify backup directory exists
      stat:
        path: "{{ backup_base_dir | default('/var/satellite-backups') }}"
      register: backup_dir_test

    - name: Record backup directory test result
      set_fact:
        test_results: "{{ test_results + [{'test': 'Backup Directory Exists', 'status': 'PASS' if backup_dir_test.stat.exists else 'FAIL'}] }}"

    - name: Find latest backup manifest
      find:
        paths: "{{ backup_base_dir | default('/var/satellite-backups') }}"
        patterns: "backup_manifest.txt"
        recurse: yes
      register: manifest_search
      when: backup_dir_test.stat.exists

    - name: Record backup manifest test result
      set_fact:
        test_results: "{{ test_results + [{'test': 'Backup Manifest Found', 'status': 'PASS' if backup_dir_test.stat.exists and manifest_search.matched | default(0) > 0 else 'FAIL'}] }}"

    - name: Test 4 - Verify patch log directory exists
      stat:
        path: /var/log/satellite-patches
      register: patch_log_test

    - name: Record patch log directory test result
      set_fact:
        test_results: "{{ test_results + [{'test': 'Patch Log Directory Exists', 'status': 'PASS' if patch_log_test.stat.exists else 'FAIL'}] }}"

    - name: Test 5 - Verify inventory group membership
      assert:
        that:
          - groups['satellite_servers'] is defined
          - groups['satellite_servers'] | length > 0
        fail_msg: "No hosts found in satellite_servers group"
        success_msg: "satellite_servers group is populated"
      register: inventory_test
      failed_when: false

    - name: Record inventory test result
      set_fact:
        test_results: "{{ test_results + [{'test': 'Inventory Group Membership', 'status': 'PASS' if inventory_test is success else 'FAIL'}] }}"

    - name: Display full test report
      debug:
        msg: "{{ item.test }}: {{ item.status }}"
      loop: "{{ test_results }}"

    - name: Count failed tests
      set_fact:
        failed_test_count: "{{ test_results | selectattr('status', 'equalto', 'FAIL') | list | length }}"

    - name: Display overall test summary
      debug:
        msg: "Test suite complete: {{ test_results | length }} tests run, {{ failed_test_count }} failed."
EOF
Subtask 4.2: Run the Test Suite and Interpret Results
Execute the testing playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/test_automation.yml
Review the output. Each debug message under "Display full test report" shows one test name paired with PASS or FAIL. The final task reports the total number of tests run and how many failed.

If any test reports FAIL, re-run the corresponding playbook from Task 1, 2, or 3 first, then re-run the test suite:

ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/satellite_health_check.yml
ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/satellite_backup.yml
ansible-playbook -i inventory/satellite_hosts playbooks/test_automation.yml
Deliverable: A completed test run whose final debug message reads Test suite complete: 5 tests run, 0 failed. (or, in a simulated environment where some services genuinely do not exist, a report clearly identifying which named test failed and why, which is itself a valid, checkable result demonstrating the test framework works correctly).

Expected Outcomes
A structured Ansible project (~/satellite-automation) containing inventory, group variables, templates, and four working playbooks covering health checks, backups, patch management, and automated testing
A verified, timestamped backup directory with a manifest file describing its contents
A passing (or clearly diagnosed) automated test report validating that the health check, backup, and patch workflows produced their expected artifacts
Troubleshooting
Symptom: ansible-playbook fails immediately with UNREACHABLE for all hosts. Diagnostic hint: Check that the inventory file path passed with -i matches inventory/satellite_hosts and that you are running commands from inside ~/satellite-automation; also confirm SSH connectivity manually with ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_rsa <user>@<host>.

Symptom: The backup playbook reports success but backup_manifest.txt is missing from the backup directory. Diagnostic hint: Check that the templates/backup_manifest.j2 file exists relative to the playbooks/ directory (the template path is relative, ../templates/...), and confirm the play has become: yes so it can write to /var/satellite-backups.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete, self-contained Ansible automation project for Red Hat Satellite maintenance, covering health checks, backup automation, patch management for both the Satellite server and its clients, and a dedicated testing playbook that validates the artifacts each workflow produces. You practiced enterprise-relevant patterns such as idempotent tasks, controlled use of failed_when, portable shell execution with explicit interpreters, retention-based cleanup, and structured test reporting using set_fact accumulation. With this foundation, you are well positioned to extend the testing framework with additional assertions, integrate these playbooks into a CI/CD pipeline or cron-based scheduler, and adapt the same structure to automate maintenance for other enterprise systems beyond Satellite.
